  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Consumption / PFCE"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Add up everything Indian households buy in a quarter, the rice and the rent, the bus fares and the school fees, the medicines and the mobile data, and you have the largest single component of GDP. The accounts call it private final consumption expenditure. Everyone else can call it household spending, and it is well over half the economy.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Private final consumption expenditure <span class="sub">PFCE · household spending · quarterly</span></div>
          <p>The value of everything households buy for their own use: food, clothing, housing services, transport, health, education, recreation. It includes the imputed value of food grown and eaten on the same farm, and it excludes house purchases, which count as investment. Growth of 7.95 per cent means households bought that much more than in the same quarter a year earlier.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the country's collective shopping bill.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">The size of the slice matters for how India grows. Investment surges and fades with credit cycles; exports rise and fall with the world's appetite. Household spending just keeps arriving, quarter after quarter, because people eat in recessions too. Economists who call Indian growth "consumption-led" are describing this arithmetic: when the biggest slice grows steadily, the total rarely strays far.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>Over half of everything.</b> PFCE is roughly 56 rupees of every 100 the economy produces. No other component comes close.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>Homes are not here.</b> A house bought is investment, not consumption. The rent, actual or imputed, is consumption.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>The quiet engine.</b> It rarely makes headlines, because it rarely surprises. That steadiness is precisely its power.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : the quiet engine -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>A decade of the collective shopping bill</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y % · quarterly</span></div>
    <p>Against the violent swings of investment, household spending draws a calmer line. Calmer is not flat. The lockdown quarter shows what happens when the shops themselves close, and the softer patches show where ordinary lives tightened before any headline admitted it.</p>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-pfce" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The quiet engine — steady, until the shops closed</h3>
            <div class="sub">Real PFCE, year-on-year · constant 2011-12 prices · Q1 FY17 – Q2 FY26</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-pfce">–</div><div class="c">Q2 FY26 · PFCE y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-pfce"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source — MoSPI, National Accounts, base 2011-12, vintage <span id="pf-pfce">–</span> · the dark rule is zero: below it, households bought less than a year earlier</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Above the zero rule, households bought more than a year earlier; below it, they bought less, and in a decade that happened in only one episode. Because the slice is so large, this line and the GDP line usually move together. When they part, ask which side is carrying the total.</p>
            <p>The crash and the spike are one event read twice. Spending collapsed when the shops closed, then printed <b id="ht-peak">–</b> a year later against the fallen base. Neither figure describes how households felt; both describe what the arithmetic did.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The FY23 case</span>
            <p>In the second half of FY23 the engine idled. PFCE growth slipped to <b id="fy23-run">–</b>, the weakest two quarters of the post-pandemic years, even as GDP held near its trend on the strength of investment and government spending.</p>
            <p>The headline said the economy was fine. The shopping bill said households were tired. Both were true, and the shopping bill was the earlier news.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 what the slice tells you -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Where a slowdown shows first</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Reading the slice</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The print · Q2 FY26</div><div class="v acc" id="n-last">–</div><div class="d">real PFCE, year-on-year</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Share of GDP</div><div class="v" id="n-share">–</div><div class="d">the biggest slice · Q2 FY26</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The lockdown quarter</div><div class="v" id="n-trough">–</div><div class="d">Q1 FY21 · the shops closed</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Pre-pandemic average</div><div class="v" id="n-trend">–</div><div class="d">FY17 to FY20</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">A slowdown in ordinary lives shows here first, long before it reaches the GDP headline. Households cut back quietly: smaller brands, postponed purchases, one meal out fewer. None of it announces itself. But the sum of a hundred million such decisions is this series, and the series speaks. The last four quarters read <b id="rec-run">–</b>, close to the pre-pandemic average, which says the cutting back has eased.</p>
    <p>One caution travels with the number. PFCE is a total, and a total of household spending says nothing about how the spending is spread. The same print can describe tight budgets in one district and easy ones in another; the accounts cannot see inside the average. For that, the household surveys are the only honest source, and they arrive years apart.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Consumption is frivolous spending.”</h3>
          <p>Most of the basket is necessity: food, housing, transport, medicine, school fees. The discretionary edge, the meals out and the new phones, is the small visible tip. When PFCE slows, it is rarely the treats that vanish first in the data; it is the necessities bought cheaper.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“If GDP rises, consumption must be rising for everyone.”</h3>
          <p>Two leaps in one sentence. GDP can rise on investment or exports while consumption idles, as it did in FY23. And consumption rising in total says nothing about every household: the average can climb while the median family cuts back. The aggregate is not the neighbourhood.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Savings don't matter.”</h3>
          <p>Income is either spent or saved, and the saved part is what funds tomorrow's investment and cushions the next bad year. A household sector that spends every rupee is not thriving; it is exposed. The savings rate beside the consumption rate completes the picture.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Quarterly estimates of private final consumption expenditure at constant 2011-12 prices; year-on-year growth computed from the published levels, vintage 2026-08-04. The GDP share uses the published constant-price levels.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>PFCE covers household spending on goods and services, including imputed rent of owner-occupied homes and own-account farm produce. House purchases sit in investment, not here. The distribution of spending across households is not visible in these accounts; the page says so rather than guessing.</p>
    <p>Quarters of the past two years are provisional estimates and will be revised.</p>
  </div>
